\startchapter[reference=sec:pub-summary, title={Summary of contributed articles}]

This chapter contains summaries of the articles to which
I made substantial contributions during my work as a Ph.D. candidate.
I will reference the aspects of frameworks established in \insec[sec:org],
and how they are serviced by my contributions:
\emph{interoperability}, \emph{interpretability}, \emph{reproducibility}, and \emph{performance}.

In general, I have contributed to a class of changes that scientists are adopting in their approach to scRNA-Seq analysis.
My contributions focused on improving the ability of scientists to apply the theoretical steps outlined in \insec[sec:workflow] to real data in praxis.
I worked on every computational step of the scRNA-Seq analysis process described in \insec[sec:workflow] except for alignment, with a specific focus on steps involving a count matrix.

My contributions to the field of scRNA-Seq data analysis include research, development, and extension of algorithms, data structures, pipelines, frameworks, and APIs.

\startsection[title={\titlecite{angerer2016}}]

Summary:
In this article, we described the initial scope of \n{destiny}, an R package implementing diffusion maps.
Diffusion maps were chosen for their at the time unique ability to capture smooth nonlinear transitions in single-cell transcription data and their robustness in the presence of noise.
\n{Destiny} was the first user-friendly and practical implementation of the algorithm, suited for being used both interactively by data scientists and by other packages.
Its practicality is a result of high \emph{performance} making it scale from personal computers to high-performance machines, as well as algorithmic enhancements adapting the algorithm to scRNA-Seq and qPCR data.

% efficiency
\n{Destiny}’s scalability and computational efficiency are mainly achieved by reusing the sparsity structure of the sparse distance matrix to perform element-wise operations.
To this end, \n{destiny} symmetrizes its kNN matrix and uses the symmetric variant of diffusion maps.
Other adaptations for efficiency include initializing based on a k-nearest-neighbor search, full sparse matrix support for input data, as well as taking advantage of an approximate Eigendecomposition algorithm.
This efficient design has been adopted by \n{scanpy} for its many tools that use a kNN search as preprocessing step.

% enhancements
\n{Destiny} also efficiently reuses intermediate results such as the transition matrix for its other functions.
Those include diffusion pseudo time (\n{DPT}, \inbracecite[haghverdi2016]) and the ability to project a second set of data into an existing diffusion map.
Apart from the sparsity support making it suitable for scRNA-Seq, explicit support for missing and censored values also allow \n{destiny} to create \emph{interpretable} results when applied to qPCR data.
The R package is continuously maintained and has been adapted to make use of current best practices such as an adaptable diffusion map kernel, plotting with \n{ggplot2} and storing expression data in the \n{SingleCellExperiment} data structure.

% user friendly: defaults, no sigma
This \emph{interoperability} and flexibility make \n{destiny}n especially user-friendly.
It is also easy to use without detailed knowledge by being able to run without specifying parameters.
To this end, \n{destiny} locally scales diffusion maps and heuristically derives a fitting number of nearest neighbors.
This nonparametric diffusion map variant is also used by \n{scanpy} today.
When opting for a global scale instead, \n{destiny} will derive a suitable kernel width by default.

% plotting and color maps
\n{Destiny} contains dedicated plotting routines for diffusion maps and \n{DPT} that directly integrate into the popular plotting package \n{ggplot2}.
Destiny started providing perceptually continuous color maps before they have been included in the major plotting packages of Python and R.
Without them, accurate \emph{interpretability} of any kind of color-mapped visualizations cannot be guaranteed \cite[borkin2011,moreland2017].
\n{Destiny} supports \n{Bioconductor}’s annotated expression matrix classes as well as plain matrices and data frames.

% flexibility
\n{Destiny} can utilize several distance metrics including euclidean, cosine, and rank correlation distance.
Unlike other available R tools, \n{destiny} uses the chosen distance metric also for kNN search, which has been shown to improve results \cite[hu2016].
\n{Destiny} can be used fully deterministically and therefore \emph{reproducibly} by setting a random seed.
\n{Destiny}’s API is flexible enough to be used as an analysis tool in its own right, as well as as a diffusion map backend for integrated analysis pipelines like \n{scater} \cite[mccarthy2017].
It also served as a flexible base for the implementation of \n{DPT} and gene relevance (see below).

Destiny has been cited 175 times at the time of writing and has for example been used for dimension reduction and pseudo-time inference in analyses.
This includes usage on hematopoietic stem and progenitor cell differentiation \cite[nestorowa2016], an immune atlas of clear cell renal cell carcinoma \cite[chevrier2017], development in the human adult brain \cite[lake2018] and an atlas of the aging drosophila brain \cite[davie2018].
\TODO[move all the italic terms into the section below?]

My contribution:
For the tool, I started with a basic diffusion map implementation from \inlinecite[haghverdi2015].
It already supported missing and censored values, while I developed all scRNA-Seq-related extensions and implemented the first published version by myself.
I implemented all further enhancements of the tool.
I conceived all optimizations improving its memory and time efficiency and therefore enabling its use on a personal computer, where less efficient tools needed to be run on a powerful server.
\n{Destiny} initially supported raw standard sparse matrix data structures and later gained support for \n{SingleCellExperiment}.
As a consequence, \n{destiny} became one of the first scRNA-Seq analysis tools able to handle sparse matrices and therefore millions of cells on a personal computer.
I helped Laleh Haghverdi in developing the nonparametric diffusion map variant during the parallel development of \n{destiny} and \n{DPT}, paving the way for \n{scanpy}’s use of the same.
I chose to default to perceptually continuous color maps, which put \n{destiny} ahead of its time in guaranteeing accurate assessment of information encoded into plot colors.
I contributed to a few of \n{destiny}’s dependencies to support random seeds for determinism.
For the publication, I wrote the first draft, which I then completed together with other contributors.
I designed the main figure to which Maren Büttner contributed cell identity annotations, and created all supplementary figures.

\stopsection

\startsection[title={\titlecite{angerer2017}}]

Summary:
In this review article, we provide a selective view of the history of single-cell data,
focusing on the growing number of captured cells per transcriptomics experiment.
We evaluate the reasons for capturing more cells, the qualitative changes in the captured data, and the consequences of these changes in terms of a shift in where challenges lie.
Capturing a high number of cells provided the necessary resolution to identify certain rare subpopulations, as well as for accurate inference of developmental trajectories.
As sequencing depth decreases just as cell numbers increase, data size per experiment does not change much.
However, tools need to be able to handle the changing data requirements, such as expression matrices stored in a sparse format, and increasing noise per datum.
Challenges can be approached with a variety of attack angles, which usually range from the emulation of older shapes, such as imputing noisy sparse data to make it smooth and dense, to approaches modeling the new properties.
The challenges can change rapidly: while smaller scRNA-Seq require regularization to handle their intrinsic “small n large p” properties, newer experiments capturing 10s of thousands of cells already no longer suffer from this problem.

My contribution:
Starting from an idea by Fabian Theis, I performed the data analysis supporting the publication’s primary thesis,
showing conclusively the growth of scRNA-Seq data in both the volume per experiment and the number of experiments.
I designed and produced the figures with feedback from other contributors.
I wrote the major part of the publication, with parts contributed by other contributors.
