\startsection[reference=sec:pub-scanpy, title={\titlecite{wolf2018}}]

Summary:
% reusable and remix-able workflow building blocks and data structures
In this article, we introduced \n{scanpy}, a framework for single-cell analysis and visualization.
\n{Scanpy} is a framework for scRNA-Seq preprocessing, analysis, and visualization in Python.
\n{AnnData} is a data structure for annotated observation data including scRNA-Seq counts and expressions.
It is designed to be generic and independent of \n{scanpy}.

\n{Scanpy} is designed around \n{AnnData}, storing the results of each processing step in an expression matrix layer or an aligned annotation (see \infig[fig:anndata]).
\n{AnnData} can be stored on disk in various formats for snapshots, data interchange, and \emph{interoperability} with other frameworks, languages, and command line tools.
Its structure allows serialization in the file-tree-like formats \n{HDF5} \cite[folk2011] and \n{Zarr} \cite[miles2022].
They, unlike Python’s “pickle” format, are not bound to individual implementation versions and therefore allow for reliable long-term storage.
For data too large to hold in memory, \n{AnnData} supports disk-backed operations on its \n{HDF5}-based \type{.h5ad} files.
\n{Scanpy} has official Docker containers and version logging facilities to help with \emph{reproducibility}.
\n{AnnData} has built-in backward compatibility with older versions of itself for the same purpose.
For details about the implementation of \n{AnnData} and \n{scanpy}, see \in{Sections}[sec:anndata] and \in{}[sec:scanpy]

\n{AnnData}’s and \n{scanpy}’s modular and flexible design philosophies allow \n{scanpy} routines to be effortlessly interspersed with preprocessing and analysis steps of other tools, including R tools via \n{SingleCellExperiment} \cite[lun2017a,angerer2019b].
\n{AnnData} has been used for this purpose by several scRNA-Seq analysis tools since the publication of \n{scanpy}, such as \n{scVI} \cite[lopez2018], \n{DCA} \cite[eraslan2019], \n{DESC} \cite[li2019], or \n{BBKNN} \cite[polanski2020].
\n{AnnData}’s \type{.h5ad} format is supported by visual analysis pipelines like \n{Galaxy} \cite[afgan2018] or \n{cellxgene} \cite[megill2020].
\n{Scanpy} is widely used and popular in the scRNA-Seq analysis community \cite[zappia2018].
It was named Genome Biology’s milestone article of the year 2018 \cite[genomebiology2020].
Due to \n{scanpy}’s popularity and \emph{performance}, it is used for large projects like the \n{Human Cell Atlas} \cite[regev2017], \n{Tabula Muris} \cite[schaum2018], or EMBL-EBI’s expression atlas, which uses \n{scanpy} for downstream analysis of all single-cell experiments \cite[papatheodorou2020].
The \n{Human Cell Atlas} and \n{COVID19 Cell Atlas} \cite[wilk2020] chose \n{AnnData}’s \type{.h5ad} format as their data interchange format.
At the time of writing, \n{scanpy} is cited by over 300 studies, review papers and tools.
The above-mentioned analysis tools share a community with \n{AnnData} and \n{scanpy} under the umbrella project \n{scverse}.
This results in benefits for \emph{interpretability}, since the community shares domain knowledge, helps to improve test coverage and error messages for common problems, and provides other feedback and enhancements for the projects.

My contribution:
For the Python packages,
I designed \n{AnnData} and the foundational API structure of \n{scanpy}.
With F. Alexander Wolf I implemented the initial version of both packages,
where he focused on performance while I focused on API and data structure design.
I contributed to the implementation of some algorithms included in \n{scanpy}.
I designed \n{AnnData}’s on-disk format together with F. Alexander Wolf, driving the choice of HDF5 as a reliable format, which led to the aforementioned adoption by large projects and popular tools.
I contributed to the manuscript after F. Alexander Wolf wrote the first draft.
I implemented \n{anndata2ri}, enabling in-memory conversion between \n{AnnData} and \n{SingleCellExperiment} and therefore Python’s and R’s primary scRNA-Seq environments \cite[angerer2019b].

\stopsection

\startsection[title={\titlecite{uzbas2019}}]

% flexible processing pipeline and Python package
Summary:
In this article, we developed BART-Seq, a targeted RNA-Seq protocol usable at single-cell or bulk levels.
See \inlinecite[uzbas2019] and \insec[sec:bart-seq] for BART-Seq, a transcriptomics protocol that allows the quantification of many samples at once.
It is set apart from untargeted single-cell and bulk RNA-Seq protocols due to its high sensitivity and low cost.
BART-Seq can use single cells or groups of multiple cells as samples.
Each of its samples is tagged by combining paired-end reads with pairs of computationally designed barcodes.
It is therefore possible to distinguish high numbers of samples while sensitively measuring the specified target amplicons, such as expressed genes.
An advantage of predictable barcoding over randomized barcodes is that sample identity is maintained, allowing for arbitrarily much information attached to each sample of a single batch, such as treatment labels.
BART-Seq has a unique demultiplexing and counting strategy that can be tuned for sensitivity or conservativeness (see \insec[sec:bart-seq]), improving \emph{reproducibility} and \emph{interpretability}.
The counting strategies and visualization routines for the protocol are available as a Python package allowing fine-grained programmatic access \cite[angerer2019a], helpful for \emph{interoperability} with subsequent analysis steps.
Part of this package is the BART-Seq equivalent to \n{Cell Ranger} or \n{zUMIs}:
a \n{Snakemake} pipeline \cite[koster2012] that performs all steps of the protocol to create count matrices, statistics and visualizations.

My contribution:
I designed the demultiplexing and counting strategy together with Fatma Uzbas and Micha Drukker.
I implemented the Python package and built the pipeline wrapping the package for use with minimal configuration.
For the publication, I wrote the section about demultiplexing and contributed to the remaining sections, mainly to the primer design section.
I demultiplexed the final version of all data used in the publication using my pipeline.

\stopsection

\startsection[title={\titlecite{angerer2020}}]

Summary:
% combines gene-level with cell-level analysis
In this article, we present gene relevance, a novel method for inference of the contributions individual variables have on the location of data in an embedding.
This algorithm is the latest addition to the \n{destiny} package, building upon its efficient routines and therefore sharing its \emph{performance}, \emph{reproducibility}, and \emph{interoperability} characteristics.
The method was inspired by PCA loadings, an intrinsic property of the linear dimensionality reduction method PCA.
These loadings are the linear factors scaling a variable to define its role in a principal component.
Using PCA as a dimension reduction method for expression data, therefore, allows seeing each gene’s contribution to a cell’s position in a plot.
Gene relevance generalizes the idea to make it applicable to nonlinear dimension reduction methods.
It is designed to identify genes that drive the local positioning of a cell in an embedding by calculating the differential of each gene-cell combination.
This involves estimating the partial derivatives of the gene expression with respect to the embedding location by looking at cell neighborhoods.
This allows to detection of locally relevant genes without a discrete clustering step, and therefore without data reuse as a result of using inferred cluster labels as input for gene-level analysis.
The article shows the robust derivation of both obvious candidates such as hemoglobin genes for hematopoietic development, as well as interesting but less obvious genes.
We also present an easy-to-use interface for calculating gene lists and plotting as part of the \n{destiny} package, allowing for easy \emph{interpretability}.

My contribution:
The initial idea comes from Fabian Theis, while I designed the changes making it applicable to real data with A. Scialdone and C. Marr.
I implemented the algorithm as an extension of \n{destiny}’s data structures and API,
offering a rich and user-friendly way to interact with all gene relevance concepts, including plotting routines.
I also extended the idea to include scores for defined groups of cells (such as clusters) or the whole data for a global relevance score per gene.
The method is described in detail in \insec[sec:gene-relevance].
For the publication, I designed and performed its data analysis, which Antonio Scialdone interpreted.
I wrote the first draft, while David Fischer and Carsten Marr contributed to the mathematical description.
I completed writing the manuscript with feedback from Carsten Marr.

\stopsection

